2.1 Six source datasets
We worked with six datasets in total.
Dataset	Classification	Source method	Business key	Raw rows
Sales order lines	Transaction	ADLS / Auto Loader	order_line_id	11,412
Inventory movements	Event	ADLS / Auto Loader	movement_id	634
Sales returns	Transaction	ADLS / Auto Loader	return_id	316
Product master	Master	Azure SQL seed data	product_id	139
Store master	Master	Azure SQL seed data	store_id	148
Supplier master	Master	Azure SQL seed data	supplier_id	157

Total source records
12,806

The important part is that these datasets have different business grains.
What is grain, and why did it matter?
Grain means what one row represents.
- Sales: one row represents one order line, not necessarily one complete customer order.
- Inventory: one row represents one inventory movement event.
- Returns: one row represents one return transaction.
- Product: one row represents one product.
- Store: one row represents one store.
- Supplier: one row represents one supplier.
We used these grains to decide the primary keys, deduplication logic, joins and fact-table design.
Important source columns
Sales order lines

order_line_id, order_id, order_ts, store_id, product_id, customer_id, quantity, unit_price, discount_pct, sales_channel, order_status, tax_amount.

Inventory movements

movement_id, product_id, store_id, movement_ts, movement_type, quantity_change, reference_id, unit_cost, warehouse_id, recorded_ts.

Sales returns

return_id, order_line_id, return_ts, return_quantity, refund_amount, return_reason, inspection_status, store_id, product_id, approved_flag.

Product master

product_id, sku, product_name, category, subcategory, unit_of_measure, list_price, cost_price, supplier_id, active_flag.

Store master

store_id, store_name, city, region, store_type, opening_date, active_flag, warehouse_id, manager_code, country.

Supplier master

supplier_id, supplier_name, country, lead_time_days, payment_terms, active_flag, supplier_tier, city, category, contact_code.
