5. Day 3 – Gold layer and business-ready modelling
Day 3 completed
Day 3 was about creating business-ready data.

Silver contains cleaned entity-level data. Gold combines it into facts, dimensions, ledgers and aggregated business summaries.

We used three notebooks for Gold.
Actual notebook	Responsibility
retail_gold_layer_dim (1).ipynb	Gold dimensions
gold_layer_fact (1).ipynb	Gold facts
gold_layer_business (1).ipynb	Inventory ledger and business summaries

5.1 Why did we create dimension tables?
Dimension tables describe business entities.

For example, a Sales Fact can contain a product_id, but the business dashboard also needs the product name, category and subcategory.

Instead of repeating every product attribute in every fact row, we maintain a Product Dimension.
Gold dimension tables
capretailnew.gold.gold_dim_product
capretailnew.gold.gold_dim_store
capretailnew.gold.gold_dim_supplier


Dimension	Rows
gold_dim_product	139
gold_dim_store	148
gold_dim_supplier	157

Why did we use dimensions?
They provide descriptive attributes for business reporting.

For example:
- Sales by product category.
- Sales by store region.
- Inventory by product.
- Supplier-level analysis.
5.2 Why did we create fact tables?
Fact tables store measurable business events.

We created three primary facts.
A. Sales Fact
capretailnew.gold.gold_fact_sales


Grain:

One row per clean sales order line.

It contains the clean sales transactions and their business measures and dimension references.

Final rows:
11,319
Important measures include the sales quantity, gross sales, discount and net sales before tax.
B. Returns Fact
capretailnew.gold.gold_fact_returns


Grain:

One row per return transaction.

Final rows:

316 return transactions

We also retained the parent-linkage status so the three returns linked to quarantined sales were distinguishable from the other 313.

The two statuses were:
MATCHED_TO_CLEAN_SALES
PARENT_SALES_QUARANTINED


This is better than silently dropping those three returns.
C. Inventory Movement Fact
