This fact provides the foundation for movement-based stock analysis.
5.3 Inventory movement ledger
Notebook: gold_layer_business (1).ipynb

This notebook also created:
capretailnew.gold.gold_inventory_movement_ledger


Why did we create a ledger?
An inventory movement fact stores individual events. A ledger adds the ordered, cumulative view of those events.

For example, suppose the movement history for one product at one location is:
Movement	Quantity change	Cumulative movement
Receipt	'+20	'+20
Sale	'-5	'+15
Transfer	'-3	'+12
Adjustment	'+2	'+14

The cumulative total is calculated using a window over the ordered movement history.

We validated:
- Movement sequence was populated.
- Cumulative quantity was populated.
- No missing sequence values.
- No missing cumulative quantity values.
Important interpretation
The cumulative sum of quantity_change is the cumulative net movement.

It is not automatically the actual on-hand stock unless we also know the opening stock balance and the unit-of-measure rules.

That is why the handbook specifically calls out stock balance reconciliation and unit-of-measure controls. We should not claim those are fully established just because the ledger was created.
5.4 Daily Sales Summary
