6. Day 4 – Production readiness and orchestration
Day 4 completed
Day 4 moved our project from a collection of working notebooks towards an orchestrated data pipeline.

Until Day 3, we had implemented the data processing logic. On Day 4, we worked on making that logic repeatable, testable, maintainable and easier to operate.

The handbook identifies six main Day 4 activities:
1. Git integration and PR.
2. Databricks Workflows.
3. Automated tests.
4. Failure and replay recovery.
5. Performance benchmarking.
6. Access controls.
04_Retail_Dataset_Handbook(1).docx
6.1 Git integration and PR
We completed the Git integration/PR activity.
Why did we need Git?
Without version control, notebook changes can become difficult to track.

Git helps us:
- Maintain project versions.
- Track code changes.
- Review modifications.
- Collaborate with team members.
- Keep a record of changes before merging them.
The purpose of a PR is to review changes before they become part of the shared project version.

We marked this activity complete during Day 4.

The exact repository name, branch names and PR number are not available in the project details I can currently verify, so I won't invent them.
6.2 Databricks Workflows – pipeline orchestration
This was one of the biggest Day 4 tasks.

Before Workflows, we could run each notebook manually.

But in a real data engineering pipeline, we need to execute notebooks in the correct order, wait for dependencies and stop downstream processing when upstream tasks fail.

That is what Databricks Workflows provides.
