6.3 Automated data-quality tests
We already had the 57-check Gold validation logic.

For Day 4, we worked on making the results more structured and persistently auditable.
Why did we need automated testing?
A notebook displaying successful results is useful, but a production pipeline also needs a record of what was tested and whether it passed.

We wanted a test matrix containing:
- Test ID.
- Test description.
- Actual result.
- Expected result.
- Test status.
- Test execution timestamp.
Test-results table
We created or configured the test output to be written into:
capretailnew.gold.gold_dq_test_results


The structure was:
Column	Meaning
test_id	Unique identifier for the test
test_description	What the test checks
actual_result	Observed result
expected_result	Required result
test_status	PASS or FAIL
test_execution_timestamp	When the test ran

Why use Delta for test results?
Because Delta allows us to store the test history in a queryable table.

We can later run SQL such as:
SELECT
    test_status,
    COUNT(*) AS test_count
FROM capretailnew.gold.gold_dq_test_results
GROUP BY test_status;


We can also investigate which tests failed in a particular run.
Important difference between overwrite and append
For the business fact tables, our transformations use the intended rebuild/overwrite approach for the current dataset.

For the test history, we use append mode so that each execution adds a new set of test results instead of erasing the earlier test history.

That means repeated workflow executions can add another set of test records. This is intentional for a history table, provided the test-run identity is handled consistently.

You confirmed that this automated testing step was implemented. I have the 57-check validation output, but not a separate final screenshot of the persisted test-results table, so that is still useful evidence to retain.
6.4 Failure and recovery testing
The next task was to demonstrate that we could handle a failed task without manually editing our transformation notebooks every time.

This led to an important discussion.
The initial approach
We first discussed a notebook containing a hardcoded exception.

That would simulate a failure, but it is not a good reusable approach because someone would need to edit the notebook to enable or disable the failure.

You correctly questioned that.
The improved approach – parameter-driven failure simulation
We changed the approach to use a Databricks widget:
# DAY 4 - FAILURE RECOVERY TESTdbutils.widgets.text("simulate_failure", "false")simulate_failure = dbutils.widgets.get("simulate_failure").lower()print("Failure simulation:", simulate_failure)if simulate_failure == "true":    raise Exception(        "Simulated transient failure for recovery testing"    )print("Notebook execution successful.")print("Recovery test completed.")



Why is this better?
Because the notebook code stays unchanged.

We can control the behaviour using a task parameter.
Parameter	Behaviour
true	Intentionally throws a simulated exception
false	Executes successfully

This separates the test configuration from the notebook's actual code.
Why is this not the same as fixing a real production error?
Because this is a controlled fault-injection test.

It demonstrates that the job can encounter a failure and that we can test recovery behaviour. It does not prove that every possible production failure is automatically repaired.

We also discussed that a Repair Run may or may not let you change task parameters in the way you expect. We should not claim that it will always automatically replace the failed parameter.

Status: The parameter-driven failure-test setup was completed. We subsequently completed the idempotent replay test. The exact failed-run and repaired-run output is not available in the evidence I can verify here, so I am not marking that separate recovery execution as independently proven.
6.5 Idempotent replay test
This was another important production-readiness check.

You ran the replay test and confirmed:
Idempotent replay passed
What is idempotency?
Idempotency means that running the same pipeline again with the same source data should not produce additional duplicate business records or change the expected final results.

For example:
- First execution: Sales Fact contains 11,319 rows.
- Second execution with unchanged source data: Sales Fact should still contain 11,319 rows.
It should not become 22,638.
What did we do?
We first captured baseline counts using SQL:
SELECT 'Bronze Sales' AS table_name, COUNT(*) AS total
FROM capretailnew.bronze.bronze_sales_order_lines

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Bronze Inventory', COUNT(*)
FROM capretailnew.bronze.bronze_inventory_movements

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Bronze Returns', COUNT(*)
FROM capretailnew.bronze.bronze_sales_returns

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Gold Sales Fact', COUNT(*)
FROM capretailnew.gold.gold_fact_sales

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Gold Returns Fact', COUNT(*)
FROM capretailnew.gold.gold_fact_returns

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Gold Inventory Fact', COUNT(*)
FROM capretailnew.gold.gold_fact_inventory_movements;


Expected counts:
Table	Expected count
Bronze Sales	11,412
Bronze Inventory	634
Bronze Returns	316
Gold Sales Fact	11,319
Gold Returns Fact	316
Gold Inventory Fact	634

Then we reran the main workflow:
Retail_Sales_Inventory_Pipeline


We did not delete the Auto Loader checkpoints or source files.

Why?

Because the checkpoint is part of how Auto Loader remembers which source files have already been processed.

Deleting it would change the replay conditions and could cause previously processed files to be ingested again.
What did we prove?
The unchanged-input replay passed.

That demonstrates that our pipeline, with its existing checkpoint and overwrite-based downstream table builds, can be run again without changing the expected business counts.

Scope of this result: It proves unchanged-source replay behaviour. It does not, by itself, prove all possible late-arrival, update, correction or changed-file scenarios.
