The notebook files themselves confirm the Bronze, JDBC, Sales Silver, Inventory Silver and Returns Silver implementations.     
Why did we not put everything in one notebook?
Because each stage has a separate responsibility.

For example:
- Profiling investigates the source.
- Bronze ingests and preserves source data.
- Silver cleans and validates it.
- Gold models it for business analysis.
- Validation checks the final outputs.
- Workflows orchestrates the complete process.
Separating these responsibilities makes it easier to debug a failure, rerun an individual stage, review code and understand the pipeline.

8. The important issues we faced and how we resolved them
This is one of the most valuable parts of the project. In an interview or client presentation, explaining the problem and why you chose a particular solution demonstrates much more understanding than just listing technologies.
Problem or decision	What we did	Why
Raw sales had duplicate order-line keys	Deterministic deduplication and duplicate audit	Avoid double-counting sales while preserving evidence
Sales quantity had -999 values	Quarantined invalid records	Prevent unrealistic quantities from distorting KPIs
Sales unit price had 999999.99 values	Quarantined invalid records	Prevent unrealistic revenue calculations
Required sales attributes were missing	Applied hard-invalid and missing-status rules	Ensure Gold sales records are suitable for analysis
Missing order status initially remained in Silver	Changed the rule and quarantined the entire record	Maintain a clean Sales Fact
Returns referenced quarantined sales	Kept returns and recorded parent-link status	Preserve valid return evidence
Return timestamps preceded order timestamps	Preserved timestamps and flagged chronology	Avoid inventing corrected business facts
Inventory contained positive and negative quantities	Preserved signed quantity changes	Maintain movement direction
Different source systems required different ingestion methods	Auto Loader for ADLS, JDBC for Azure SQL	Match ingestion to source type
Re-running ingestion could duplicate data	Retained Auto Loader checkpoints and tested replay	Maintain repeatable ingestion
Hardcoded failure simulation required editing notebooks	Switched to parameter-driven fault injection	Separate test control from notebook code
Team permissions were broad	Inspected and documented them without revoking access	Avoid disrupting the shared environment

Three particularly important engineering decisions
A. Why quarantine instead of deleting records?
Because rejected records still have business and audit value.

If we simply delete an invalid sales record, we lose the ability to investigate why it was excluded.

With quarantine, we preserve it separately and keep the clean Silver and Gold datasets reliable.
B. Why did we retain the three unmatched returns?
Because the return records were intrinsically valid. The problem was that their parent sales records had been quarantined.

Deleting the returns would have hidden legitimate source activity. We instead represented their linkage condition explicitly.
C. Why didn't we correct the return timestamps?
Because we did not have a confirmed business rule establishing which timestamp was wrong.

Changing source timestamps without evidence would create fabricated data.

We preserved the original event and added a chronology flag.

